+# FAA recording custody: newly acquired source records and unresolved labels
+
+October27,2026. Existing original-record goal: AUD016-A1/TC01–TC04 and clock/custody dependencies. This unit advances recorder/radar custody, not the separate FAA corrected-answer and administrative-action PD01–PD04 chain.
+
+**Five official NARA PDFs now supply actual preservation, impoundment and later-handling records. They identify specific tapes and copies, but their conflicting labels remain unresolved and their contents do not establish substitution, destruction or a conspiracy.** A final NTSB inventory page supplies further CD/report targets. Original tape bits, full transfers and clock calibration remain missing.
+
+## New evidence, with source limits
+
+1. **A printed tape-ID discrepancy.** A September11 request lists original SAR tapes1633,1635,**1636**; a September13 account of September11 impoundment instead lists1633,1635,**1658**. Both are retained exactly. They are different documents and speech acts; we have not established that they exhaustively identify the same physical set. Do not repair1658 to1636 or conclude a tape was substituted. Original labels, completed logs, earlier copies and any correction would discriminate a clerical difference, different selection, or another cause. [NARA34916079](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34916079), PDFpp7,2; readingsR02.
+
+2. **A meaningful ID1635 connection.** Both slips retain1635; a handwritten table gives1234–1258. The previously acquired FAA native inventory independently lists that ID/window and the already held NTAP print identifies input ID1635. This supplies a document-to-media-label chain worth pursuing. It is not an authenticated physical-tape/byte join, independently identified aircraft, or calibrated clock. The NTAP header's1234:32–1258:08, query stop125800 and inventory's1234–1258UTC are preserved as distinct fields. No eight-second correction is inferred. ReadingsR03; [held inventory study](../faa-native-transfer-records-2026-10-27/README.md) and [held radar study](../radar-correlation-originals-2026-10-27/README.md).
+
+3. **Preservation and copy segregation are expressly recorded.** September11 national instructions call for two copies of0900Z–2100Z HOST SAR tapes, secured originals and signed releases. A DSR original-list instruction prohibits ordinary analysis of originals at ZID, directing use of copies instead. This is substantive contrary evidence to an assumption that no preservation controls existed. Instructions do not establish universal compliance, complete custody, current survival or release completeness. The blank transfer template in this packet is not proof that no completed counterpart exists. [NARA34916079](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34916079), pp1,8,10; [NARA7603836](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7603836), p1; R01.
+
+4. **A concrete later-handling target.** A handwritten note says tapeZI5204 was removed5/5/04 for investigation. Another page mentions a data floppy given toAT on5/11/04. Their dates are visual readings of handwriting; they are not authenticated native records. The removal note does not say original or copy, destination, return or disposition. It is a retrieval lead, not proof of theft or destruction. [NARA7603839](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7603839), pp3,2; R05. DSR identifiers must not be equated with the separate HOST1635 series merely because both are SAR-related.
+
+5. **Requested and retrieved reel dates differ.** An October8,2003 memo describes a request for September11 material, but its retrieved labels include7A and4B datedSeptember12, plus7B datedSeptember11. Three recorder-strip rows correspond to those recording labels; other DATED and period fields remain distinct and some handwriting is uncertain. This is an unresolved request/selection/date-label question. The actual reels, label photos, channel schedules, request and subsequent copy/examination log could distinguish adjacent-day media, mistaken labels, different field meanings or another cause. None has been selected as the explanation. [NARA7596600](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596600), p1; [NARA7596599](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596599), pp1–2; R06.
+
+6. **Repeated list agreement is measurable, but not independent corroboration.** Seven LCN/BCN tape/time rows match across original-list, copied-list and retention-packet pages. Arithmetic on adjacent labels yields overlaps9,10,9seconds forLCN and10,9seconds forBCN. These are nominal label overlaps, not measured duplicate returns, lost data or clock offsets. DARC0110 is a separate listed object, not an eighth LCN/BCN row. Copies of the same list do not create extra witnesses. R04; [LABELS.json](LABELS.json), [test_labels.py](test_labels.py) and [RESULTS.json](RESULTS.json).
+
+7. **The last NTSB inventory page gives exact outstanding products.** It lists two radar-dataCDs and an all-aircraft radar report as complete, while ASCE/NORAD impact-parameter rows carry different FBI release statuses. The inventory is printedFebruary21,2002; the previously acquired delivery letter is datedJuly17,2003. These dates, annotation dates, recipient receipt and current release status are different milestones. Handwritten report additions are not authenticated native enclosures. Only the last reader page is new here; earlier four pages and the official radar report were already acquired and published. [Public reader](https://www.scribd.com/document/12962758/List-of-NTSB-Documents-Provided-to-9-11-Commission), reader5, printed3of3; R07.
+
+## Acquisition, inspection and reproducibility
+
+The new source set is five agency-hosted released scans, **17 PDF pages**, plus **one third-party reader JPEG**. All18 page/image views were inspected as visible pixels. No hidden text behind redactions was recovered; original bytes were not altered. Exact URLs, actual access times, byte counts, SHA256 and source roles are in[SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json). Official hosting pins this release copy; it is not native-email, original-form authorship or physical-media authentication. NARA catalog use says unrestricted while series access notes possible restrictions/redactions; both are retained without conflation.
+
+The narrow reproducible test checks three manually transcribed seven-row lists, their adjacent-label arithmetic and the preserved printed-ID difference. It does not decode tapes or calibrate clocks. Source transcription is a disclosed input, not an independent OCR or experimental measurement. The original/copied/retention agreement must not be counted three times as historical evidence.
+
+The strongest objection to a concealment reading is that these very records explicitly direct copying, impoundment, secure retention and release documentation, and identify later handling openly. Those controls could coexist with ordinary errors or with incomplete compliance; neither history is established by an instruction sheet. Conversely, the existence of controls cannot explain away the printed-ID and selection/date differences or replace missing logs.
+
+## Original records to pursue next
+
+- HOST1633/1635/1636/1658 original labels and physical inventory; completed impoundment/release logs and any correction to the September13 slip. Preserve all four IDs until joined, rather than requesting only a preferred correction.
+- SAR1635 original/copies, extraction job package, NTAP definitions/control-card response and byte-level output correspondence. Keep the recorded time labels and query controls distinct until semantics are established.
+- DSRZI5204 removal/return and examination records, May2004 floppy and manifest, AOS/ZIDAF/WJHTC signed transfers and surviving copies. Establish system/media identity before joining it to HOST or voice reels.
+- October8,2003 request, access log, actual7A/7B/4B labels/content/channel schedules and copy worksheets; original ambiguous strips rather than a normalized date transcription.
+- NTSB two radarCDs, native production/enclosure manifest, recipient receipt, the all-aircraft study's source-file manifest and any separately titled Time Correlation Report. Actual RADES/NTAP/FDR synchronization worksheets and residuals remain unacquired.
+
+No outreach or new request was sent. These are existing-record targets, not proof that each survives or is currently withheld. The original five-track plan remains active: FAA correction/receipt and signed dispositions; native telephone/PBX and field definitions; raw recorder/radar/calibration; authenticated materials/custody/test joins; and steel preservation/transfers/attribution rationale. This custody acquisition does not complete any of those substantive tracks.
+
+## Preservation and our own process corrections
+
+Earlier reports and their original wording remain unchanged. Before the new acquisitions, two stale handoff pointers caused duplicate source navigation/searches. A global current-holdings check established that the radar study, inventory pages1–4, two native FAA DOCs and earlier transfer packets were already acquired. They are reused context, not new findings here. The mistaken initial protocol, later scope corrections and failed empty target file remain preserved locally; the valid six-target list and actual receipts identify what was genuinely acquired. Search/navigation repetition is our process overhead, not suspicious agency conduct.
+
+Only this original review, selected safe readings, source pins, transcriptions, code/results and revision checks form the public payload. Raw scans, page images, full catalog captures, contact/signature fields and internal coordination remain preserved locally. Existing sources/reports/plan are not overwritten. No private-repository push, case-record edit, fee, new filing/request, deletion or history rewrite is part of this unit. The exact additions are supplied in[diffs](diffs/README.md.diff); publication evidence is verified separately.
